WEBVTT

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.380
Welcome to the first lecture on the course on programming data structures and algorithms

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:06.940
in Python.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.360
Let's start with the basic definition of what we mean by an algorithm and what programming

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:14.720
is.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:23.440
As most of you probably know, an algorithm is a description of how to systematically

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.800
perform some task.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.410
So an algorithm consists of a sequence of steps which we can think of as a recipe

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.230
in order to achieve something.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.450
So the word recipe of course comes from cooking where we have a list of ingredients

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.790
and then a sequence of steps to prepare a dish.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:48.370
So in the same way an algorithm is a way to prepare something or to achieve a given task.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:56.750
So in the context of our thing, a recipe was what we call a program and we write down

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.250
a program using a programming language.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:05.030
So the goal of a programming language is to be able to describe the sequence of

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:11.790
steps that are required and to also describe how we might pursue different sequences of steps

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.510
if different things happen in between.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:23.960
So the notion of a step is something that can be performed by whatever is executing

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:24.580
the algorithm.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:30.020
Now a program need not be executed by a machine although that will be the typical context

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.800
of computer programming where we expect a computer to execute our steps.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.100
A program could also be executed by a person.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:44.420
For instance, supposing the task at hand is to prepare a hall for a function.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.760
So this will consist of different steps such as cleaning the room, preparing the stage,

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.080
making sure the decorations are up, arranging the chairs and so on.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.380
This will be executed by a team of people.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:05.240
Now depending on the expertise and the experience of this group of people, you can describe this

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.780
algorithm at different levels of detail.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.880
For instance, an instruction such as arrange the chairs would make sense if the people

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.880
involved know exactly what is expected.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.320
On the other hand, if this is a new group of people who have never done this before,

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.940
you might need to describe the step in more detail.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.680
For instance, you might want to say that arrange the chairs in eight rows and

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.980
put 10 chairs in each row.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.520
So the notion of a step is subjective.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.460
It depends on what we expect of the person or the machine which is executing the algorithm

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.180
and in terms of that capability, we describe the algorithm itself.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.620
Our focus in this course is going to be on computer algorithms and typically these

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.900
algorithms manipulate information.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.420
The most basic kind of algorithm that all of us are familiar with from high school

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.840
is an algorithm that computes numerical functions.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.460
For instance, we could have an algorithm which takes two numbers X and Y and computes

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:10.540
X to the power Y.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:16.340
So we have seen any number of such functions in school, for example, to compute square root

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.840
of X. So what we do in school is we have a complicated way to compute square root

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.900
of X or we might have X divided by Y where we do this long division and so on.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:31.940
So these are all algorithms which compute values given one or more numbers.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.240
They compute the output of this function.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.800
But all of us who have used computers know that many other things also fall within

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.680
the realm of computation.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:48.560
For instance, if we use a spreadsheet to arrange information and then we want

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:49.440
to sort a column.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.280
So this involves rearranging the items in the column in some order, either

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.920
an ascending order or a descending order.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.640
So reorganizing information is also a computational task.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.920
And we need to know how to do this algorithmically.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:09.890
We also see computations around us in our day-to-day lives.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:14.530
For instance, when we go to a travel booking site and we try to book a flight

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:20.230
from one city to another city, it will offer to arrange the flights in terms

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.270
of the minimum time or the minimum cost.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.390
So these are optimization problems.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:29.190
This involves also arranging information in a particular way and then computing

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:30.570
some quantity that we desire.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:35.290
In this case, we want to know A that we can get from A to B and B among

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:39.110
all the different ways we can get from A to B, we want the optimum one.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.180
And of course, there are very, very many more things that we see day-to-day

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.880
which are executed by computer programs.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.360
We can play games.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.920
For instance, we can solve Sudoku or we can play chess against a program.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:57.660
When we use a word processor to type a document or even when we use our cell

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.640
phones to type SMS messages, the computer suggests corrections in our spelling.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.300
So we will look at some of these things in this course.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:12.140
But the point is to note that a program in our context is anything

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:15.900
that looks at information and manipulates it to a given requirement.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.100
So it's not only a question of taking a number in and putting a number out.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.200
It could involve rearranging things.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.520
It could involve computing more complicated things.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.360
It could involve organizing the information in a particular way.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.420
So these computations become more tractable.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:32.900
And that is what we call a data structure.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:40.630
So to illustrate this, let us look at a function which most of us have seen

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.190
and try to understand it algorithmically.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.990
So the property that I want to compute is the greatest common

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:50.770
divisor of two positive integers m and n.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:55.330
So as we know, a divisor is a number that divides.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.670
So k is a divisor of m.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.130
If I can divide m by k and get no remainder.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:05.630
So the greatest common divisor of m and n must be something which is a common divisor.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.530
So common means it must divide both.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:10.570
And it must be the largest of these.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.830
So the largest k such that k divides m and k also divides m.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.640
For instance, if we look at the numbers 8 and 12,

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.360
then we can see that 4 is a factor of 8.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.360
4 is a divisor of 8.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.500
4 is also a divisor of 12.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:31.080
Another divisor of 12 is 6.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.960
But 6 is not a divisor of 8.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.700
So if we go through the divisors of 8 and 12,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:40.120
it's easy to check that the largest number that divides both 8 and 12 is 4.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.020
So GCD of 8 and 12 is 4.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.700
What about 18 and 25?

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.460
25 is 5 by 5.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.840
So it has only one divisor other than 1 and 25, which is 5.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.100
And 5 is not a divisor of 18.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:56.920
But fortunately, 1 is a divisor of 18.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.160
So we can say that GCD of 18 and 25 is 1.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.860
There is no number bigger than 1 that divides both 18 and 25.

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:11.100
Since 1 divides every number, as we saw in the case of 18 and 25,

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.380
there will always be at least one common divisor among the two numbers.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.420
So the GCD will always be well-defined.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.780
It will never be that we cannot find a common divisor.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:25.420
And because all the common divisors will be numbers,

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:27.480
we can arrange them from smallest to largest

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.740
and pick the largest one as the greatest common divisor.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.560
So given any pair of positive numbers m and n,

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.700
we can definitely compute the GCD of these two numbers.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.380
So how would one go about computing GCD of m and n?

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.160
So this is where we come to the algorithmic bit.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.540
We want to describe a uniform way

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:55.660
of systematically computing GCD of m and n for any m and any n.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.100
So here is a very simple procedure.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.500
It's not the most efficient.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.200
We will see better ones as we go along.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.820
But if we just look at the definition of GCD,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.260
it says look at all the factors of n,

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:09.860
look at all the factors of n,

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.140
and find the largest one, which is a factor of both.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.620
So the naive way to do this would be to first list out

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:17.180
all the factors of the first number m.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.920
Then list out all the factors of the second number n.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.540
And then among these two lists report the largest number

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.660
that appears in both lists.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.940
This is almost literally the definition of GCD.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.680
Now the question is does this constitute an algorithm?

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.380
Well at a high level of detail,

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.800
if we think of list out factors as a single step,

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.640
what we want from an algorithm are two things.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.120
One is that the description of what to do

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.380
must be written down in a finite way.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:54.920
In the sense that I should be able to write down the instructions

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.540
regardless of the values of m and n in such a way

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.680
that you can read it and comprehend it once and for all.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.580
So here it's very clear we have exactly three steps.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.560
So we have three steps that constitute the algorithm.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.800
So it's certainly presented in a finite way.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.040
The other requirement of an algorithm

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.460
is that we must get the answer after a finite number of steps.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.280
Now this finite number of steps may be different

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:20.980
for different values of m and n.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.480
You can imagine that if you have a very small number for n,

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:25.860
there are not many factors.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.820
If you have a very large number for n, you might have many factors.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:32.080
So the process of listing out the factors of m and n

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:33.400
may take a long time.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.640
However, we want to be guaranteed that this process will always end

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.300
and then having done this, we will always be able to find

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.240
the largest number that appears in both lists.

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:50.330
So to argue that this process is well defined,

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:56.090
all we need to realize is that the factors of m must be between 1 and m.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:59.270
In other words, although there are infinitely many

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:01.350
different possibilities as factors,

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:03.370
we don't have to look at any number bigger than m

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:04.710
because it cannot go into m even.

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:09.290
So all we need to do to compute the factors of m

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.010
is to test every number in the range 1 to m

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:15.090
and if it divides m without a remainder,

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:16.970
then we add it to the list of factors.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.190
So we start with an empty list of factors

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.750
and we consider in turn 1, 2, 3, 4 up to m.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:24.990
And for each such number, we check whether

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:27.750
if we divide m by this number, we get a remainder of 0.

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:30.490
We get a remainder of 0, we add it to the list.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:34.010
So let us look at a concrete example.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.610
Let us try to compute the GCT of 14 and 63.

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:40.410
So the first step in our algorithm says

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.230
to compute the factors of 14.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:46.370
So by our observation above, the factors of 14

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:48.050
must lie between 1 and 14.

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:49.890
Nothing bigger than 14 can be a factor.

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:52.490
So we start by listing out all the possible factors

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:54.310
between 1 and 14 and testing them.

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:57.570
So we know of course that 1 will always divide.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:01.590
In this case, 2 divides 14 because 14 divided by 2 is 7

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:02.270
with no remainder.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.290
Now 3 does not divide, 4 does not divide,

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:11.130
5 does not divide, 6 does not divide, but 7 does

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:14.970
because if we divide 14 by 7, we get a remainder of 0.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:20.230
Then again 8 does not divide, 9 does not divide and so on.

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.690
And finally we find that the only other factor left is 14 itself.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.750
So for every number m, 1 and m will be factors

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:28.450
and then there may be factors in between.

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:32.790
So having done this, we have now identified the factors of 14

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:36.930
and these factors are precisely 1, 2, 7 and 14.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:43.560
So the next step in computing the GCD of 14 and 63

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.360
is to compute the factors of 63.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:49.300
So in the same way, we write down all the numbers from 1 to 63

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:50.940
and we check which ones divide.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.860
So again we will find that 1 divides, here 2 does not divide

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.820
because 63 is not even, 3 does divide.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.200
Then we will find a bunch of numbers here which don't divide

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.640
and then we will find that 7 divides because 7, 9s are 63.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:08.180
Then again 8 does not divide, but 9 does.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.340
Then again there are a large gap of numbers which don't divide

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:15.540
and then 21 does divide because 21, 3s are 63.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:19.460
And then finally we find that the last factor that we have is 63.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.120
So if we go through this systematically from 1 to 63,

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.140
crossing out each number which is not a factor,

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:30.880
we end up with the list 1, 3, 7, 9, 21 and 63.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:36.160
Having computed the factors of the two numbers, 14 and 63,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:39.840
the next step in our algorithm says that we must find

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.160
the largest factor that appears in both lists.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:43.840
So how do we do this?

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.260
How do we construct a list of common factors?

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:49.880
Now there are more clever ways to do this,

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:51.840
but here is a very simple way.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.080
We just go through one of the lists,

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.360
say the list of factors of 14 and for each item in the list,

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.300
we check if it is a factor of 63.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:04.400
So we start with 1 and we say it does 1 appear as a factor of 63,

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.520
it does, so we add it to the list of common factors.

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:10.580
Then we look at 2 and then we ask does it appear,

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:12.240
it does not appear, so we skip it.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:19.480
Then we look at 3 and look at 7 rather and we find that 7 does appear,

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:20.820
so we add 7.

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.540
Then finally we look at 14 and find that 14 does not appear,

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:25.200
so we skip it.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:33.600
So in this way we have systematically gone through 1, 2, 7 and 14

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:37.360
and concluded that of these only 1 and 7 appear in both lists.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:42.060
And now having done this we have a list of all the common factors,

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:45.460
we computed them from smallest to biggest

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:48.320
because we went through the factors of 14 in ascending order.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:50.620
So this list will also be in ascending order.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:56.520
So returning the largest factor just returns the rightmost factor in this list namely 7.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:58.840
So this is the output of our function.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:02.720
So we have computed the factors of 14, computed the factors of 63,

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:06.960
systematically checked for every factor of 14 whether it is also a factor of 63

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.680
and computed this list of common factors here and then from this list

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:14.800
we have extracted the largest one and this in fact is our GCT.

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.800
So this is an example of how this algorithm would execute.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:28.900
So if you were to write it down in a little more detail then we could say that we need to

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.260
notice that we need to remember these lists and then come back to them.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:38.040
So we need to compute the factors of 14, keep it aside, we need to write it down somewhere.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:42.300
We need to compute the factors of 63, write it down somewhere and then compare these two lists.

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.640
So in other words we need to assign some names to store these.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:53.460
So let us call fm for factors of m and fm for factors of n as the names of these lists.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:15:03.620
So what we do is that we run through the numbers 1 to m and for each i in this list 1 to m we check

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:08.960
whether i divides n, whether m divided by i has remained a 0 and if so we added to the list

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:15.600
factors of m or fm. Similarly for each j from 1 to n we check whether j divides n

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:22.960
and if so we add it to the list fm. Now we have two lists fm and fn which are the factors of m

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:28.860
and the factors of n. Now we want to compute the list of common factors which we will call

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:36.080
cf. So what we do is for every f that is a factor of the first number, remember in our case it was

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:43.240
14 for each f so we ran through 1, 2, 7 and 14 in our case. So for each f in this list

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:48.080
we add f to the list of common factors if it also appears in the other list. So in the other list

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:56.580
if you remember it was 1, 3, 7, 9, 21 and 63. So we compare f with this list and if we find

00:15:56.580 --> 00:16:03.560
it we add it to cf. And having done this now we want to return the largest value in this list

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:07.940
of common factors. Remember that 1 will always be a common factor so the list cf will not

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:13.940
be empty so there will be at least one value. But since we add them in ascending order since

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:20.380
the list fm and fn were constructed from 1 to m and 1 to n the largest value will also be the

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:27.060
right most value. So this gives us a slightly more detailed algorithm for GCD. It is more or

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:31.080
less the same as the previous one except it spells out in little more detail how to compute

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:35.860
the list of factors of fm, how to compute the list of factors of n and how to compute

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:40.520
the largest common factor between these two lists. So earlier we had three abstract statements now we

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:49.500
have expanded it out into six slightly more detailed statements. So this already gives us

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:55.280
enough information to write our first Python program. Of course we will need to learn a little

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:59.120
more before we know how to write it but we can certainly figure out how to read it.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:04.960
So what this Python program is doing is exactly what we described informally in the

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:10.980
previous step. So the first thing in the Python program is a line which defines the function.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:17.720
So we are defining a function GCD of m, n. So m and n are the two arguments which could be any

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:22.660
number like any function it's like when you write f of x, y in mathematics it means x and y are

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:27.140
arbitrary values and for every x and y do something depending on the values that you

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:32.860
call the function with. So this says that this is our definition so def for definition

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:45.540
of a function GCD m, n. So now the first step is to compute the list of factors of m.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:54.860
In Python we write a list using square brackets. So list is written as x, y, z and so on.

00:17:55.540 --> 00:18:00.660
So the empty list is just an open bracket and a square close bracket. So we start off with an

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:12.840
empty list of factors. So this equality means assign a value. So we assign fm the list of factors of

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:18.800
m to be the empty list. Now we need to test every value in the range 1 to n.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:26.340
Now Python has a built-in function called range but because of we shall see because of

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:32.400
a peculiarity of Python this returns not the range you expect but one less. So if I say

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:36.800
give me the numbers in the range 1 to n plus 1 it gives me numbers in the range 1, 2,

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:43.200
m, 1 up to the upper limit but not including the upper limit. So this will say that i takes the

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:50.160
values 1, 2, 3 up to m. For each of these values of i we check whether this is true.

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:57.900
Now percentage is the remainder operation. So it checks whether remainder of

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:06.040
m divided by i is 0. If the remainder of m divided by i is 0 then we will append i to the list fm.

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:11.220
We will add it to the right. Append is the English word which just means add to the end of the list.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:17.380
So we append i to m. So in this step we have computed fm. This is exactly what we wrote

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:24.700
informally in the previous example. We just said that for each i from 1 to m add i to fm

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:30.460
if i divides n and now we have done it in Python syntax. So we have defined an empty list of factors

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:34.580
and for each number in that range we have checked if it is a divisor and then added it.

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:39.660
And now here we do the exactly the same thing for m. So we start with the empty list

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:47.340
of factors of n for every j in this range. If it divides we append it and now at this point

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:54.400
we have two lists fm and fm. And now we want to compute the list of common factors.

00:19:54.820 --> 00:20:00.960
So we use cf to denote the list of common factors. Initially there are no common factors.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:06.840
Now for every factor in the first list if the factor appears in the second list then

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:10.840
we append it to cf. So the same function append is being used throughout.

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:16.000
It says take a list and add a value. Which value? We add the value that we are looking

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:21.100
at now provided it satisfies a condition. So earlier we were adding it provided the divisor was 0

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:25.620
or the remainder was 0. Now we are adding it provided it appears in both lists for every f

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:30.360
in the first list if it appears in the second list added. So after this we have computed ff.

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:43.120
And now we want the rightmost element. So this is just some Python syntax which you will see

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:47.560
which says that instead of if you start counting from the left then the numbers the

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:52.940
positions in the list are numbered 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. But Python has the shortcut which says if you

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:57.200
want to count from the right then we count the numbers as minus 1, minus 2 and so on. So it says

00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:03.080
return the minus 1th element of cf which in Python jargon means return the rightmost element.

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:13.940
So this is the rightmost element. So at this point it's enough to understand

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:21.460
that we can actually try and decode this code this program even though we may not understand

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:25.920
exactly why we are using colons in some places and why we are pushing some things.

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:30.540
So you notice that there are other syntactic things here. So there are for example you have

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:35.520
these punctuation marks which are a bit odd like these colons. Then you have the fact that

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:40.860
this line is indented with respect to this line. This line is indented with respect to this

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:46.320
line. So these are all features that make Python programs a little easier to read and

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:50.520
write than programs and other languages. So we will come to these when we learn

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:56.580
Python syntax more formally but at this point you should be able to convince yourself that this

00:21:57.140 --> 00:22:04.280
set of Python steps is a very faithful rendering of the informal algorithm that we

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:13.720
wrote in the previous slide. So let us note some points that we can already deduce from this

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:20.620
particular example. So the first important point is that we need a way to keep track of

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:25.740
intermediate values. So we have two names to begin with the names of our arguments m and n.

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:31.060
Then we use these three names to compute these lists of factors and common factors

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:38.400
and we use other names like i, j and f in order to run through these. We need i to run from one

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:44.400
to n. We need j to run from one to n. Of course, we could reuse i but it is okay. We use f to run

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:49.040
through all the factors in cf. So these are all ways of keeping track of intermediate values.

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:56.640
The second point to note is that a value can be a single item. For example, m, n are numbers.

00:22:56.980 --> 00:23:01.820
Similarly, i, j and f at each step are numbers. So these could be single values

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:08.540
or they could be collections. So there are lists. So fm is a list, fn is a list. So it is a single

00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:13.320
name denoting a collection of values. In this case, a list, a sequence. It has a first position,

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:18.760
an exposition and a last position. So these are lists of numbers. One can imagine other

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:23.440
collections and we will see them as we go along. So collections are important because it

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:27.860
would be very difficult to write a program if we had to keep producing a name for every

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:31.780
factor of m separately. We need a name collectively for all the factors of m,

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:39.560
regardless of how big m is. So these names can denote single values or collections of values.

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:44.320
And a collection of values with a particular structure is precisely what we call a data

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:50.140
structure. So these are more generally called data structures. So in this case, the data

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:58.720
structure that we have is a list. So what can we do with these names and values? Well, one

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:04.840
thing is we can assign a value to a name. So for instance, when we write fn is equal to the

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:10.940
empty list, we are explicitly setting the value of fn to be the empty list. This tells two things.

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:14.660
It says the value is an empty list. It also tells Python that fn denotes a list. So these are,

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:19.800
there are two steps going on here as we will see. And the other part is that when we write

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:26.420
something like for each f in the list cf, this is implicitly saying that take every value

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:30.820
in cf and assign it one by one to the value to the name f. So though we do not have this

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:36.480
equality sign explicitly, implicitly this is assigning a new value for f as we step through

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:41.720
the list cf. So the main thing that we do in a Python program is to assign values to names.

00:24:45.280 --> 00:24:49.800
And having assigned a value, we can then modify the value. For instance, every time

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:55.060
we find a new factor of n, we do not want to throw away the old factor. We want to take

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:59.420
the existing list fn and we want to add it. So this function append for instance,

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:05.780
modifies the value of the name fn to a new name, which takes the old name and sticks an i at the

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:10.400
end of it. So more generally, we could have a number and we could want to replace it by two

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:18.160
times a number. So we might have something like i is equal to two times i. So star stands for

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:22.320
multiplication. This does not mean that i is equal to two times i arithmetically,

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:27.280
because obviously unless i is zero, i cannot be equal to two times itself. What this means is take

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:32.420
the current value of i multiplied by two and assign it to i. So we will see this as we go along. But

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:38.660
assignment can either assign a completely new value or it could update the value using the

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:43.580
old value. So here we are taking the old value of the function of the list fn and we are

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:50.460
appending a value to it and getting a new value of fn. The other part that we need to note

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:55.340
is how we execute steps. So as we said at the beginning of today's lecture,

00:25:55.740 --> 00:26:00.240
a program is a sequence of steps but we do not just execute a sequence of steps from

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:06.040
beginning to end blindly. Sometimes we have to do the same thing again and again. For instance,

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:12.960
we have to check for every possible factor from 1 to m if it divides m and then put it in the

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:18.460
list. So some steps are repeated, we do something for example here for each item in a list

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:24.760
and some steps are executed only if the value that we are looking at meets a particular condition.

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:34.340
So when we say something like if m percent i is zero, if the remainder of m divided by zero then

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:44.460
append, so the step append i to fm, the factors of m, this happens only if i matches

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:51.780
the condition that it's a factor of m. So we have repeated steps, same thing done again and again

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:56.860
and we have conditional steps, something which is done only if a particular condition holds.

00:26:57.580 --> 00:27:01.220
So we will stop here. So this example should show you that

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:06.700
programs are not very different from what we know intuitively. It's only a question of

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:11.140
writing them down correctly and making sure that we keep track of all the intermediate

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:15.160
values and steps that we need as we go along so that we don't lose things.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:19.360
We will look at this example in more detail as we go along and try to find other ways

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:23.880
of writing it and examine other features but essentially this is a good way of illustrating

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:24.260
program.

